PART I: THE SEVEN THOUSAND STEPS

The steps began where the town ended, on the far side of the bridge — gray slabs worn smooth, dented down the middle by seven thousand years of pilgrims climbing them up and down. They went up together, the whole company: Ismara, Orion, Ylva, Nora, Bjorn, Mila, GEAR, the horses, Alfonso dormant and carried. Nobody left anything behind that hadn't already been hidden — the cart locked in the stable, crystal now, and unlikely to interest anyone who found it.

The first stones were warm under their boots despite the cold, sun caught in something that wasn't quite sun. A few turns up, a stone tablet stood at a right angle in the stair — old script, readable. Before the birth of men, the dragons ruled all Mundus. Their word was the Voice, and they spoke only for true need, for the Voice could blot out the sky and flood the land.

The wrongness started small. Snow near their boots fell upward before settling back down, ordinary again a moment later. Then it wasn't small: Ismara noticed Mila had grown — an inch, more, in the time it took to blink — and pressed her Sight against her to check. No unusual aura. Nothing marking her as touched by anything Ismara could name. She turned the same look on Bjorn out of habit, and found nothing there either — not "nothing wrong," nothing at all, no trace of whatever was happening to everyone else in his direction. She asked him what he was. He didn't understand the question. He didn't know he wasn't aging with the rest of them; he had no way to know it without eyes like hers.

Ismara pressed anyway — sharper than she needed to, sharper than usual — and Bjorn had no idea why the woman wearing Davinia's face wouldn't leave him alone. He answered what he could. Told her Mila had good color from the road, working hard on that soup every night, cabbage in it more nights than not, and it seemed to be doing her good. He didn't have the first idea what she meant by aura or magic surrounding her — none of that was language that meant anything to him. Someone showed him his own reflection so he'd see it too. He looked no different to himself.

Ismara's suspicion crystallized into something the group could act on: whatever was happening ran on contact. Mila and the black mare, Balthazar, were aging at exactly the same rate — the two of them, and nothing else, moving in lockstep through it while everyone else drifted at their own pace. If touch bound people to the same rate, then binding themselves together bought protection from drifting apart. They tied the party together with rope, hand to hand, and kept climbing.

PART II: THE GUARDIAN

The line between them had already worked loose, unnoticed, by the time the path bent hard around a shoulder of rock — a narrow track with a fall on one side and open stone climbing away on the other. Four robed figures stood waiting where the bend opened, a fifth held back behind them. Ismara knew the robes on sight: the same ones worn by the Mythic Dawn cultist they'd found frozen in crystal, root-locked beneath the heart tree at the Midden. These didn't sound friendly, and didn't wait long enough to prove otherwise.

GEAR called up its ice-cased guardian to meet them and tried to read what they actually were — the horn found nothing to hold onto. They weren't solid enough to resonate at any frequency, nothing stable enough to name a strength or a weakness against. All GEAR could confirm was the plainest thing: eleven points of damage resistance, a hundred each, no tell beyond that.

Orion threw Alfonso bodily at the nearest of them, meaning to run him straight through. The cultist blinked out and caught the blade out of the air bare-handed instead — and where his skin met the rapier, something crept up his arm, hesitated, and crept back down again, refused. He dropped Alfonso rather than carry whatever that had almost been.

Ylva went in with the axe Alfonso had made her, lightning riding the swing, and put one of them down hard enough that the strike carried through to whoever stood beside him. Varon tried his own weapon's trick next — the edge that was supposed to cut straight past armor and answer only to flesh — and found nothing there to answer it. Whatever these things were built from, it wasn't meat.

The cultists fought like nothing else the company had crossed blades with before: pulling extra turns out of time itself, the same way the mountain had already been doing to the party all day, burning their own strength to buy the extra strike. It worked, and it cost them — every time one of them reached for a second action that way, it came back out of their own hide, twenty points at a stroke, spent on speed instead of armor.

It went hard on both sides. Davinia went down once and came back up on Saijah's healing; Ylva dropped hard enough to cross her own threshold and rose again mid-fight as something else entirely, claws first, driving two of them back with a single wide arc before the shape settled again. Varon pushed himself past what his body wanted to give twice over, buying the extra turns in the same coin the mountain had already been charging everyone all day. Twenty years came off him at once, nowhere else to spend them but forward. Mila, still climbing with them and still innocent of any of it, had crossed into her tenth year sometime in the middle of the exchange, and hadn't slowed down to notice.

The fight was still going when they stopped — four cultists battered but standing, the fifth untouched and waiting at the back. Nothing about it was finished yet.
